Pick a random starting point within the first interval, then take every k-th item after it. To audit every twentieth voucher in a register, start at a random voucher among the first twenty and continue in steps of twenty.

Three things to remember:

- every item has an equal chance of selection, **but not every possible sample does**: items next to each other can never be picked together;
- it is quick and often cheaper than simple random sampling;
- it can mislead when the interval lines up with a **pattern** in the list. Sampling every seventh day of cash withdrawals could pick only pay-days, for example.

The **trend** is the long-run direction of a series, ignoring the ups and downs around it. There are three reasons to measure it:

- to **describe** history and judge whether past policies worked;
- to **project** the future, such as expected growth in deposits or GDP;
- to **remove** it, so that seasonal and cyclical movements show up cleanly.

A trend may be a straight line (linear) or curved (curvilinear). A new product, for example, grows slowly, then fast, then levels off or declines, which no single straight line describes.

When growth speeds up or slows down, a straight line fits badly. Add a squared term: y-hat = a + bx + cx squared. With coded time (so Sum x = 0 and Sum x cubed = 0) three equations pin down the constants:

- b = Sum xy / Sum x squared;
- Sum y = n a + c Sum x squared;
- Sum x squared y = a Sum x squared + c Sum x to the fourth.

Solve the last two together for a and c. The sign of c tells you the curve's shape: positive bends upward (accelerating), negative bends downward.

The normal curve is a symmetric bell. Its centre is the mean, which also equals the median and the mode, and the total area under it is 1. Two numbers fix it: the mean mu and the standard deviation sigma. About half of the data lie on each side of the mean.

To find a probability, **standardise**: Z = (X - mu) / sigma tells you how many standard deviations X is from the mean. Then use the standard normal table of areas between 0 and z:

- values on opposite sides of the mean: add the two areas;
- values on the same side: subtract the smaller from the larger;
- tails: take 0.5 minus the area from 0 to z.

To decide in advance how much to expect to lose on a loan, combine three estimates:

- **EAD**, exposure at default: how much is owed when default happens;
- **PD**, probability of default: how likely default is over the period;
- **LGD**, loss given default: the **share** of the exposure that would be lost (not recovered).

Expected loss is PD times EAD times LGD. If you are told the **recovery rate** instead, LGD = 1 - recovery rate.

Example: EAD Rs 35,00,000, PD 0.20 per cent, LGD 57 per cent gives 0.0020 x 35,00,000 x 0.57 = Rs 3,990.

**Minimising cost** works the same way, with the signs reversed: a **positive** Z-row entry now signals potential improvement, the entering variable is the one with the **largest positive** entry, and the leaving variable is again chosen by the smallest positive ratio. In the graphical method, evaluate the cost at each corner and take the smallest.

Beyond one-off allocations, linear programming is used for:

- **sensitivity analysis**: how profit changes if costs or resource availability change;
- **goal programming**: building several desired goals into the objective, for break-even analysis and financial decisions;
- **financial budgeting**: representing accounts in matrix form in a spreadsheet and planning across several periods.

**HRD** (as the courseware, quoting Rao and Pereira, describes it) is a process by which employees are helped, **continuously and in a planned way**, to:

1. acquire or sharpen the capabilities needed for **present or expected future jobs**;
2. develop their **general capabilities** and discover their inner potential;
3. build a **culture** of strong supervisor-subordinate relations, teamwork and collaboration among units;
4. develop **competencies**: knowledge, attitudes and skills in technical, managerial, human relations and conceptual areas.

**Goals** reach from the **individual** (present role, future roles) to the **dyad** (employee and supervisor), the **team**, **collaboration between units**, and the organisation's **health and self-renewing capability**.

**HRD systems:** learning and development; performance appraisal, feedback and counselling; potential appraisal, career planning and counselling; organisational development; the HR information system; talent management and succession planning.

HRM as a whole has three subsystems: **administrative, developmental and maintenance**; HRD is the developmental one.

**Which loans:** all **floating rate personal or retail loans** (housing, auto and so on) and all floating rate loans to **micro, small and medium enterprises** must be tied to an **External Benchmark Rate**. Banks may offer such loans to other borrowers too.

**Which benchmarks** (the External Benchmark Rate includes):

- the **RBI policy repo rate**;
- the **Government of India 3-month Treasury Bill yield** (published by FBIL);
- the **6-month Treasury Bill yield** (published by FBIL);
- any other benchmark **market interest rate published by FBIL**.

**Uniformity:** a bank must use **one external benchmark per loan category**: no mix of benchmarks within a category, for transparency and standardisation.

**Reset:** at least **once in three months**.

The courseware adds why there is no monthly publication duty: the bank has **no control** over an external benchmark, and linking to it lets **policy rates transmit** to borrowers.

If a company has a **director** (other than a **nominee director** of a government or financial institution put on at the time of distress) whose name appears **more than once** in the **list of wilful defaulters**:

- the provision on its **standard** accounts is **5 per cent**;
- if the account becomes an **NPA**, it attracts **accelerated provisioning**, at higher rates than the regular ones.

For **every loan proposal** (new or existing borrower) the bank needs the borrower's **financial details**. The statements are the **backbone** of the credit proposal: the credit decision rests on them.

They are the **formal record** of a business's financial activity and show its condition in the **short, medium and long term**. Analysis lets the banker judge:

- **liquidity**;
- **long term solvency**;
- **financial viability**;
- **profitability**.

**Ratio analysis** shows whether the business is **improving or deteriorating** over the years and allows **comparison with other entities**.

Under the Companies Act 2013 (courseware account, section 2(40)) the financial statements are a **set**:

1. **Balance sheet** at the end of the year;
2. **Profit and loss account** (an **income and expenditure account** for a not-for-profit activity);
3. **Cash flow statement**;
4. **Statement of changes in equity**, if applicable;
5. **Explanatory notes** annexed to or forming part of these.

A **one person company, small company, dormant company and start-up** may **leave out** the cash flow statement.

Where a company has **subsidiaries, associates or joint ventures**, it must also prepare **consolidated** statements (section 129(3)) in the same form as its own.

**Users:** lenders, creditors, investors, government departments and agencies, rating agencies, customers, employees, the general public and analysts.

**Management prepares, the auditor opines.** The auditor does not guarantee the figures, so a lender analyses them itself.

**Genuineness checks by the credit officer** at sanction, renewal and review:

- compare the audited financials with those on the **Ministry of Corporate Affairs website**;
- if they are not there, get **independent confirmation from the CA firm** and **record it in the proposal**;
- **cross-check** major profit and loss and balance sheet items with statutory and regulatory returns;
- read the statements **together with the auditor's report and its notes**.

**Supporting reports:** the **Directors' Report** (results, future plan, dividend, responsibility statement, accounting standards, true and fair view, books and fraud safeguards, going concern, internal financial control, compliance) and the **auditor's report**, an opinion on true and fair representation, with the **Companies (Auditor's Report) Order, 2020** as the statutory reporting format.

**Weaker sections** overlaps the other categories. Priority sector loans to these count:

- **small and marginal farmers**;
- **artisans, village and cottage industries** with individual limits up to **Rs 2 lakh**;
- beneficiaries of **NRLM**-type government schemes and the **manual scavenger rehabilitation scheme**;
- **Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes**;
- **DRI scheme** beneficiaries;
- **SHGs and JLGs** and their members with qualifying microfinance loans;
- **individual women** up to **Rs 2 lakh** per borrower (the Rs 2 lakh cap does not apply to UCBs);
- **distressed farmers**, and other **distressed persons** up to **Rs 1 lakh**;
- **persons with disabilities** and **transgenders**;
- **notified minority communities**.

**PMJDY overdrafts** within DFS limits may be counted. Where a notified minority is in **majority in a State or Union Territory** (Punjab, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Lakshadweep, Jammu and Kashmir), only the **other notified minorities** count.

**Sexual harassment** under the PoSH Act 2013 is **any one or more** of these **unwelcome** acts or behaviours, direct or by implication:

1. **physical contact and advances**;
2. a **demand or request for sexual favours**;
3. **sexually coloured remarks**;
4. **showing pornography**;
5. any other unwelcome **physical, verbal or non-verbal conduct of a sexual nature**.

**Who is protected:** an **aggrieved woman** is **any woman, of any age, employed or not**, who alleges harassment at a workplace. Cover reaches the **organised and unorganised** sectors and **domestic workers**.

**Who is an employee:** regular, temporary, ad hoc and daily wage staff, directly or through an agent, including **volunteers, trainees, contract workers, apprentices, consultants and unpaid interns**.

**The cycle:** cash buys **raw materials**, which become **stock in process**, then **finished goods**, which are sold to create **book debts** (receivables), which are collected as **cash**, and the cycle repeats.

**How long the cycle is depends on:**

1. **raw material stock** held;
2. **process time** (work in process);
3. **finished goods** stock before sale;
4. **credit given to purchasers**.

**Longer cycle = more working capital needed.** Getting the cycle right is the most important step in assessing gross working capital, NWC and bank finance.

**Liquidity ratios:** the banker stipulates a **minimum NWC**, which fixes a **minimum current ratio (above one)** that must be kept **at all times**. It goes into the **sanction terms** and is a **monitoring tool** for the use of funds.

**Policy:** the credit policy covers **types of facility, limits, appraisal, security, fraud prevention, monitoring, delegation and audit**.

**Who may get an NFB facility:** only a **customer with a funded credit facility from the bank**. **Exceptions:**

1. **derivative** contracts with a counterparty;
2. **partial credit enhancement**;
3. facilities on the **counter guarantee of another regulated entity**;
4. an obligor with **no fund based facility from any regulated entity** in India;
5. facilities against a **No Objection Certificate** from the entity giving the fund based facility;
6. facilities **fully secured by eligible financial collateral**.

**Banned:** an NFB facility **assuring redemption or repayment** of funds raised through **deposits, bonds or otherwise**, unless specifically permitted.

**Devolvement:** when an NFB facility **devolves** and becomes fund based, **fund based prudential norms** apply.

**A bank guarantee is, in general**

- **irrevocable**: no clause lets the bank cancel it;
- **unconditional**: nothing prevents timely payment if the counterparty fails;
- **incontrovertible**, with a clear way to **honour it without demur**.

The bank **honours it when invoked** under its terms **unless a court order restrains** it.

**Controls:** internal **aggregate and individual ceilings**, especially for **unsecured guarantees**; policy on **invocation, claim period, tenor, fees, release of security, renewal, fraud prevention**.

**Guarantee favouring another regulated entity:** **not allowed** to enable it to give a **fund based** credit facility, unless RBI permits (**trade related** guarantees are excepted). A guarantee for another entity's **NFB** facility is allowed and counts as an **exposure on the obligor**.

**Stock and commodity brokers:** only **scheduled commercial banks** may guarantee in favour of exchanges in lieu of **security deposit or margin**, as the exchange rules accept.

**Co-acceptance of bills**

- only **genuine trade bills**; goods **actually received** in the borrower's stock accounts;
- **records** of each customer's co-accepted bills, covered by **internal audit**;
- **never** co-accept bills drawn by **another lender**, or where buyer or seller has had **funding** for the transaction from **any lender**.

Infrastructure projects have **very long implementation, gestation and pay back periods** and a **high debt-equity ratio**. Their **long-term deployment** of funds reduces the lender's reinvestment risk. The **basic appraisal principles are the same**, with **extra points** to consider.

**Five broad heads** (as the courseware lists, from a Gazette notification; the master list has since been revised):

1. **Transport** (roads, bridges, ports, airports, railway track);
2. **Energy** (generation, transmission, distribution, pipelines);
3. **Water and sanitation**;
4. **Communication** (telecommunication);
5. **Social and commercial infrastructure** (education, hospitals, certain hotels, industrial park and agriculture infrastructure).

**Wilful default** by a borrower: a **default in payment** to the lender **plus any one or more** of:

1. the borrower **has the capacity** to pay;
2. the borrower **diverted** the funds;
3. the borrower **siphoned off** the funds;
4. the borrower **disposed of assets** given as security **without the lender's approval**;
5. the borrower or promoter **failed to infuse equity** despite the ability, though the lender gave loans or concessions on that commitment.

**A guarantor** is in wilful default if it does **not honour the guarantee when invoked** despite sufficient means, or sells secured assets without approval, or fails an equity commitment.

| Term | Threshold and meaning |
| --- | --- |
| **Wilful defaulter** | Wilful default with outstanding **Rs 25 lakh and above**; for a company, its **promoters and directors** (subject to the non-executive director rule); for other entities, the persons **in charge of management** |
| **Large defaulter** | Outstanding **Rs 1 crore and above** and the account is **suit-filed** or **doubtful or loss** |

**Diversion** includes: short-term working capital for long-term use; funds for activities or assets not sanctioned; transfers to **subsidiaries or group firms** without approval; **routing through another bank** without permission; **investing in other companies** without approval; **unaccounted shortfall** in deployment.

**Siphoning:** funds used for purposes **unrelated to the borrower's operations**: a judgement based on **objective facts**.

**Liquidation starts when**

1. **No resolution plan** is received before the process period (or the section 12 maximum) ends;
2. the Authority **rejects a plan** for non-compliance;
3. the **CoC decides to liquidate** by **66 per cent**, at **any time before confirmation of a plan** (even before the information memorandum);
4. the corporate debtor **contravenes an approved plan** and an affected person applies.

**The order:** the Authority orders **liquidation**, issues a **public announcement**, and sends the order to the **registering authority**.

**The liquidator (courseware, needs_review):** usually the **RP** unless ineligible; announces appointment and invites claims within **5 days**; **stakeholders' list in 45 days**; **consultation committee in 60 days**; **asset memorandum and preliminary report in 75 days**; **quarterly progress reports**; liquidates within **one year** (sale period can add **90 days**); then a **final report** and an application for **dissolution**.

**Dissolution:** on the Authority's order the corporate debtor **ceases to exist**; a copy goes to the **registrar within 7 days**.

The policy is **approved by the Board**. It must spell out:

1. the bank's **compliance philosophy**;
2. **expectations on compliance culture**: **tone from the top**, **accountability**, **incentive structure**, **effective communication and challenge**;
3. the **structure and role of the compliance function**;
4. the **role of the CCO**;
5. the **processes** to **identify, assess, monitor, manage and report** compliance risk throughout the bank.

**Other requirements**

- Reflect the bank's **size, complexity and risk profile**.
- Cover **statutes, rules, regulations, codes (including voluntary ones)** and the bank's **own internal rules**; create a **disincentive structure** for breaches.
- A **quality assurance and improvement programme** for all aspects of compliance, with **independent external review at least once in three years**.
- Special thrust on **compliance culture** and on **vetting the quality of compliance reports to RBI** by top executives, the **Chairman** and the **ACB**.
- **Reviewed at least once a year.**

Held at needs_review (courseware, from the 2007 guidelines).

The function works under a **compliance programme**: **planned**, **risk-based**, **overseen by the head of compliance**. It may carry statutory roles such as **anti-money laundering officer**.

**Annual compliance risk assessment** and plan, covering:

1. **compliance failures** and steps to avoid recurrence;
2. **major regulatory guidelines** issued in the year and steps taken;
3. **independence** and **scope** of the function;
4. **procedures** and the **system of internal control**;
5. **fair practices codes**, **self-regulatory standards**, **accounting standards**;
6. **rectification** of deficiencies in **internal audit, statutory and RBI inspection** reports;
7. the **strategy for next year**.

**Reports:** **monthly** to **senior management or CEO**; **quarterly** brief to the **Board, ACB or Board Committee**; **material failures reported promptly**.

**Other points:** **time-bound strategy** for the **Monitorable Action Plan / Risk Mitigation Plan**; **annual internal audit review** of the compliance function; written **guidance and manuals**; for **cross-border business**, structure follows **local law**, with **local expertise** and **oversight from the head of compliance**.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

**In the RBI's risk based supervision (Inspection for Supervisory Evaluation, under SPARC)** the CCO:

1. **co-ordinates with Head Office departments** for **information and data**;
2. arranges the **exit meeting** with the bank's **top executives**;
3. co-ordinates the **meeting of the top executive with RBI top management**;
4. **presents the Risk Assessment Report, Risk Mitigation Plans and supervisory rating** to the **Board or committees** at once;
5. **submits the action plan and compliance** on the RAR and RMP to the **Board** for approval and to **RBI** until compliance is accepted;
6. gives **Inspection and Audit** the points where RBI accepted compliance, so **sustainability** can be checked;
7. reports **compliance status and emerging issues** at branches to top management and the **ACB or Board**.

**Other functions:** formulate and update **compliance rules** with functional departments on **KYC, AML and CFT**, **deposits and services**, **advances** and **FEMA**.

Fraud usually grows where **controls are weak** and **governance** is poor. So internal audit:

1. includes **fraud risk** in the **audit plan** and in **each assignment**;
2. lets the **Head of Internal Audit** assess how fraud risk is managed and the bank's **exposure**;
3. trains auditors to spot **red flags**, understand **fraud techniques** and judge whether an **investigation** is needed;
4. does **not investigate** fraud itself unless it has the **expertise**, and avoids **conflicts of interest**.

**Escalation:** all **pending high and medium risk paragraphs** go to the **Board or ACB**. If management has accepted a risk the bank may not be able to bear, the **Head of Internal Audit** takes it up with **senior management** and, if unresolved, the **Board or Committee**.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

**Board:** sees that the audit function is **positioned, staffed and equipped** to work **independently**; audit reports must reach the **Board or ACB without management filtering**; it **delegates** to the **ACB** the audit framework and the review of policies and controls.

**ACB (as the courseware describes it):**

- chair is **independent** and **not the Board chair**; members include people with **audit and financial reporting** experience;
- oversees **financial reporting**, recommends **external auditors** and their pay, approves **audit scope and frequency**, follows up **corrective action**;
- reviews **annual statements with the management letter**, **audit reports** and **fraud and cash shortage reports**;
- meets the **Head of internal audit** and management; may hire **experts** at the bank's cost;
- meets **at least quarterly**; a resolution needs **two thirds** of members, and a **dissenting** member of a carried resolution gives a **written statement**.

**Why disclose:** so markets can judge **capital adequacy**, **risk exposures**, **risk assessment** and **key business parameters**. Disclosures **add to**, and do not replace, other legal reporting.

**Who is covered (2025 Directions, in force at the cut-off):** **Commercial Banks** only, meaning banking companies other than **RRBs, small finance banks, payments banks and local area banks**, plus corresponding new banks and **SBI**. The 2021 Master Direction the courseware describes was **repealed**.

**Consolidated financial statements (CFS):**

- prepared by every such bank, **listed or not**, alongside the standalone statements;
- sent to the **Department of Supervision, RBI, within one month** of publishing the accounts;
- consolidate **all subsidiaries, domestic and foreign**, unless the accounting standards allow exclusion, with **reasons disclosed**;
- an **RRB** is treated as an **associate** in its **sponsor bank's** CFS.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

A listed bank must **warn the exchanges in advance** of a board meeting that will consider:

1. **financial results** (quarterly, half-yearly or annual);
2. a **buyback**;
3. **voluntary delisting**;
4. **fund raising** by any method, including fixing the **issue price**;
5. **dividend** or **convertible securities** (including debentures with a right to subscribe to equity);
6. any change in the **form or nature** of listed securities, or in the **date, interest or redemption amount** of debentures and bonds.

Intimation also precedes an **AGM, EGM or postal ballot** to approve fund raising.

**Held back:** the **number of days** of notice. The courseware's 2, 5 and 11 day schedule is not tested because a 2024 amendment is reported to have simplified it, and the current text could not be confirmed.

**Structure, top to bottom:** **Board** → **Audit Committee of the Board (ACB)** → **MD or executive director** → an **independent Compliance department** headed by the **CCO** → compliance staff at the **corporate office verticals** → **field offices** (**Field General Manager's offices** where they exist, **regional**, **zonal** offices, **branches**).

**Who reports to whom:**

- the **CCO** reports directly to the **MD and CEO** and/or the **Board or ACB**; if to the MD and CEO, the **ACB meets the CCO quarterly one-to-one**, without senior management;
- **business and functional departments** report to the **CCO** on compliance;
- **Compliance Officers at regional and zonal offices** report to the **CCO** directly;
- the CCO **reviews the performance of every Compliance Officer**; the **Board or ACB** reviews the **CCO's** appraisal.

**Choosing the CCO:** a **senior-level selection committee** set up by the **Board** recommends names **in order of merit**; the **Board decides**. **RBI's Department of Supervision, Central Office** is told **beforehand** of an appointment, premature transfer or removal, with the **profile**, the **fit and proper certificate** by the **MD and CEO** and the **reasons** for any change.

**With the compliance staff, senior management must:**

1. **apprise** the Board and senior management of **rules and developments**;
2. **clarify** compliance questions;
3. **assess compliance risk at least yearly** and plan activity; the **plan goes to the ACB** and is **available to internal audit**;
4. **report promptly** on major changes;
5. **report failures and breaches** periodically to the Board or ACB and circulate to functional heads;
6. **test compliance** with representative testing and report the results;
7. check **sustenance** of compliance;
8. ensure **RBI's supervisory observations** are met **in letter and spirit, on time and sustainably**;
9. (courseware) identify the **main compliance risks** each year and plan to manage them.

Held at needs_review (courseware). Main functions:

- **identify** which business functions each **guideline** touches and **assess risk** by line, product and process;
- **pass on** regulator and **Ministry of Finance** instructions promptly and get **conclusive compliance** reported to the **ACB quarterly**;
- sit on the **Product Group Committee** to **vet new products** from the compliance angle and **monitor new products intensively for six months**;
- give **inspectors and concurrent auditors a checklist**; **test compliance** on a **sample basis in selected zones each quarter**, reporting to the **MD and CEO**;
- coordinate with **RBI's Senior Supervisory Manager team** in **risk based supervision** (data for **Tranches I, IA, IB, II and III**, reviewed at **DGM or AGM level**);
- coordinate **returns and replies** to RBI and guide on **cross-border** business.

The **CCO is the nodal contact with RBI**. Directions from **IRDA, SEBI and others** go to **separate compliance officers**; any **discomfort** voiced by another regulator is **taken to RBI through the CCO**.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

Head office **functional departments** run **their own controls** and **report breaches promptly** to the Compliance department. Each **functional head designates a senior officer (minimum scale IV or V)** as the department's **compliance officer**, who:

- **lists compliance issues** from guidelines and sends them to the Compliance department;
- **acts on** issues the department flags and **monitors and reports**;
- keeps **registers of returns**, ensures **timely regulatory returns** and that **agendas go to the Board, ACB and the Supervisory Committee on Risk**;
- turns guidelines into **actionable instructions** (technology flags, exception reports, job cards, manuals, training).

**The department also:** builds **compliance culture**; **vets the quality and integrity** of data going to RBI; holds **prime responsibility** for monitoring compliance in its area; **updates manuals and codes of conduct** (on the bank's website); forwards **RBI and MOF communications** for action; and sends a **quarterly compliance certificate** to the Compliance department. The Compliance department gives the **ACB a quarterly note** confirming compliance with RBI and MOF circulars.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

**Branch:** the **front line unit** of the compliance hierarchy. **Conflict of interest** between managing compliance risk and business growth cannot be fully avoided, so it must be **kept from getting in the way**. The **branch manager, accountant and officers** are primarily responsible; the **branch manager is also the branch's compliance officer**.

**FGMO, zonal and regional offices:** **compliance officers** are designated there and **report directly to the CCO** and apprise the local head of **compliance levels and breaches**. These offices:

1. report to the **central compliance department**;
2. send a **quarterly status** of the whole command area in a prescribed format;
3. **report and escalate breaches immediately**;
4. have the **Internal Control and Inspection department** cover compliance in **inspections**;
5. act in **handholding, trouble shooting and monitoring** for branches.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

Each **centre covering a cluster of foreign branches or representative offices** has an **independent Compliance department**:

- headed by a **local compliance officer**, preferably or together with an **India-based officer in Scale III or IV**, supported by local staff;
- the centre's compliance officer has a **dotted line to the CCO**, monitors **all branches in the centre**, sends **monthly reports**, and does **compliance testing (preferably quarterly)** reported independently to the CCO;
- the **chief executive of the centre** implements the **bank's policy or the local guidelines, whichever is more stringent**;
- a **dedicated overseas compliance desk at head office** gives oversight and ensures timely reporting to **top management, ACB, Board and RBI**.

Held as the courseware's account (Basel Committee framework).

**Objectives:** efficient and effective operations; accurate records; reliable financial reporting; effective risk management systems; compliance with laws, regulations and internal policies. Controls remain exposed to **execution risk** because people run them.

**Typical controls:** **limit authorities**; **safeguard access** to records and assets; **separate and rotate duties**; **regular and unscheduled reviews** including testing.

**Five components:**

1. **Control environment** (structure, management philosophy, integrity and competence, external influences, Board and committee attention, HR policies);
2. **Risk assessment**;
3. **Control activities**;
4. **Accounting, information and communication systems**;
5. **Self-assessment or monitoring**.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

**Culture:** compliance goes **beyond what is legally binding** to **integrity and ethics**, shaped by **tone from the top, incentive structure, accountability, communication, transparency, awareness and controls**.

**Five customer rights compliance protects:** **Fair Treatment**; **Transparency, Fair and Honest Dealing**; **Suitability**; **Privacy**; **Grievance Redress and Compensation**.

**RBI's expectations (risk based supervision):**

- **business, risk management, compliance and internal audit** are the **three lines of defence**;
- the **Board** puts **policies** in place documenting the **purpose, authority and responsibility** of the oversight functions;
- they are **independent** of business with a **co-operative** relationship, have the **same stature**, enough **experienced and qualified staff**;
- the Board keeps a **quality assurance and improvement programme** with **external assessment**;
- **senior management must not interfere** with their independence;
- **compliance is a core activity and must not be outsourced**.

**Basel Committee definition (courseware):** the risk of **legal or regulatory sanctions, material financial loss or loss to reputation** from failure to comply with **laws, regulations, rules, self-regulatory standards and codes of conduct**.

**Common compliance risks:** corruption, employee behaviour, workplace health and safety, environmental impact, data management, quality, process, social responsibility.

**The level of compliance risk in each business line, product and process rests on:**

1. **regulatory focus**;
2. **customer service** aspect;
3. **control** aspects;
4. **nature of activity** (inherently high risk?);
5. the bank's **exposure** (materiality);
6. **past major breaches** (history);
7. **penalty** implications.

**Other points:** a **new product approval process** puts **compliance mitigants in place before launch** and the **compliance officer sits on the approval committee**; the **CCO** joins the **quarterly informal discussions with RBI**; **staff accountability** is examined for every failure and **compliance record counts in performance appraisal**.

The courseware states that, as part of **corporate governance**, the compliance function is:

- **independent and sufficiently resourced**;
- **clearly defined** in its responsibilities;
- **subject to periodic independent review by internal audit**;
- a **core risk management activity that is not outsourced**.

The RBI circular of **11 September 2020** supports the independent review: the compliance function is **internally audited**, and the **quality assurance programme** is **externally reviewed at least once in three years**.

Held at needs_review (courseware). **Credit audit** is a **mid-course correction** after sanction.

**Objectives:** better **credit quality**; review the **sanction process and compliance of large loans**; **regulatory compliance feedback**; **independent review of credit risk assessment**; **early warning signals**; corrective action on **credit quality, administration and skills**.

**Home:** a **specific department** or the **Inspection and Audit department**. Functions: process reports, analyse findings, follow up, **apprise top management**, **arrange closure**, keep a **database**.

**Scope:** portfolio review (concentrations against the loan policy and RBI norms) and **loan review**:

- **all fresh proposals and renewals** within **3 to 6 months** of sanction;
- **existing accounts at or above a cut-off** limit;
- a **random 5 to 10%** of the rest;
- **sister, group and associate concerns** even if below the cut-off.

**Frequency by risk:** **high risk: 3 months**; **average: 6 months**; **low: 1 year**.

**Procedure:** **on site at the branch that appraised the advance** and holds the main operative limits; report on the **conduct of all allocated limits** by calling for details from corresponding branches; auditors **need not visit borrowers' premises**.

